Patch 7908 (ENHANCEMENT): players choose the player order: a device-to-player table in cellPad, started by a chord or the port, off until used.

Players choose the player order instead of the host slot: cellPad keeps a device-to-player table (libs/input/pad_join.h).

Not in force until a reorder is started, so the old rules (XInput slot order, INPUT_MODE, PAD_PAD_P2) stay exactly as they were.
Started by pad_join_request() (the port's Port Settings row) or by the chord Select + L3 + R3 held 2 s on any pad; PAD_JOIN=0 turns
both off. Then every device (XInput slot, the DualSense stand-in = slot 0, the keyboard) takes the lowest free player at its first
button press, up to four; the joining press is swallowed until its device was released. While join runs the game keeps seeing the
layout it had before (the old default layout, or the previous table), minus devices whose joining press is still held; the new table
takes over in one step when join ends: every device that must join has, four are taken, or a new Start from player 1. After that a
device that disconnects frees its player, a later one takes the lowest free player, and player 1 is never left empty while another
player exists (the lowest other player moves up). A device that is still holding a button from before join or from plugging in is
neutral until it was released. The keyboard is a device only where INPUT_MODE gives it a player (keyboard / kbm, controller with
PAD_PAD_P2, or no pad on slot 0); it holds the end of join open only in keyboard / kbm mode or when no pad is connected.

Each poll now composes the four ports in a local buffer and publishes each once; the reports count as fresh only after that (a reader
that lost the pad lock no longer sees a half-built one). ASSIGN_CHANGES is raised on every port whose device changed after a
reorder. Rumble goes to the device the game sees on the port; a motor is stopped when its device leaves that port and at join start.
g_pad_join_notify(joining, next_player) tells the port when to draw its on-screen text; it is called outside the pad lock, one
call at a time, with generations in order.

The chord and the keyboard rules read the wall clock only to time a held chord; the game never sees it.

Pure logic: installer/tools/pad_join_test.c (host test, no pad needed). Not tested with real pads.

